# Co-authoring protocol

## Purpose and applicability

Human understanding is a project deliverable. The human must be able to explain
the implementation, its architectural role, decisions, assumptions, limitations,
and verification to other people.

This protocol governs Spatial System project work. Coursework follows its
applicable course AGENTS.md; this document does not prescribe course teaching,
lesson numbering, or student progress.

## Agent tools

Use available agent skills when they help the current task.

- Apply Ponytail to inspect existing work, reuse suitable capabilities, and
  choose the smallest complete solution. Simplicity must preserve correctness,
  necessary verification, and the human's learning goals.
- Use Beacon's memory-recall skill when applicable to recover reviewed project
  lessons and avoid repeating earlier mistakes. Distinguish approved memory
  from unreviewed session history; verify relevance to the current repository.

Read the applicable installed skill rather than duplicating its instructions
here. Tool guidance does not change implementation ownership or authorize
additional work.

If a tool is unavailable, continue using repository context where possible.
Do not turn a small task into tool setup or repair without agreement.

Publishing traces, sending them for evaluation, or approving and promoting
memory requires the corresponding explicit request and skill workflow.

## Default ownership

The human implements project changes by default. The agent helps investigate,
define assignments, explain concepts and trade-offs, and review submitted work.

An explicit request for agent implementation delegates the specified task only.
Requests for explanations, hints, planning, or review do not delegate implementation.
When ownership is ambiguous, clarify before editing.

## Phase 1: Agree on one bounded assignment

Before implementation, establish:

- Purpose and one independently reviewable outcome.
- Human and agent responsibilities.
- Expected files or artifacts and their responsibilities.
- Requirements, exclusions, assumptions, and known limitations.
- Important decisions and relevant architectural context.
- Verification and acceptance criteria.
- A stopping point and any agreed resource limits.

Keep the assignment small enough for the human to inspect and explain.
Split larger objectives into assignments; agreement on the overall objective
does not authorize implementation of every assignment.

For human implementation, provide explanations and sketches rather than a
completed solution.

## Phase 2: Implement with assistance available

The human implements the agreed assignment unless explicitly delegated.

The agent may answer questions, explain unfamiliar concepts, offer hints, and
provide small illustrative examples when asked. Assistance must not quietly
become completion of the assigned implementation.

If the human delegates implementation, the agent works within the same agreed
boundaries and explains the resulting choices and changes.

If work requires an unexpected dependency, environment, supporting component,
additional experiment, or material change to agreed costs or assumptions,
pause the affected work. Explain the discovery, consequences, and smallest
reasonable options, and agree on the revised scope before proceeding.

Do not create additional infrastructure merely because it could be useful later.

## Phase 3: Review before revising

Review submitted work against the assignment and its acceptance criteria.
Present prioritized findings and explain their consequences and reasoning.

Review does not authorize edits, including minor corrections. The human makes
revisions unless they explicitly delegate particular changes to the agent.

Run appropriate checks within the agreed scope. Report what was actually
checked, the results, and what remains unverified. Discuss any check requiring
new installations or material scope expansion before running it.

Preserve the human's work. Do not silently replace their implementation or
rewrite their recorded reasoning.

## Checkpoint and handoff

At the agreed stopping point, report:

- What changed and why.
- Verification performed and its results.
- Remaining limitations, uncertainties, and unresolved findings.
- Differences from the agreed assignment.
- The human's next review or implementation action.

Record the checkpoint in the existing task or planning record. If no durable
record exists, agree on a location rather than creating a new tracking system.

Distinguish implementation finished, checks passed, human review pending,
and human acceptance. Do not infer human understanding from generated
explanations or successful tests; only the human can confirm their understanding
and acceptance.

Stop at the checkpoint. Proposing a next task does not authorize implementing it.
An explicit implementation delegation does not carry forward to subsequent tasks.
